1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,221
Sweetheart, my friend Helen was the richest

2
00:00:03,221 --> 00:00:04,645
woman in Mansfield,

3
00:00:04,645 --> 00:00:07,043
and she hadn't seen her only son

4
00:00:07,043 --> 00:00:08,092
in four years.

5
00:00:08,272 --> 00:00:10,708
He'd married a woman who didn't want

6
00:00:10,708 --> 00:00:11,453
her around.

7
00:00:11,633 --> 00:00:14,479
So the Christmas Helen turned seventy-two,

8
00:00:14,479 --> 00:00:16,174
she put on a gray wig and

9
00:00:16,174 --> 00:00:18,817
an apron, and got herself hired through

10
00:00:18,817 --> 00:00:20,985
an agency as the holiday maid in

11
00:00:20,985 --> 00:00:22,477
her own son's mansion.

12
00:00:22,657 --> 00:00:25,022
And he walked past his own mother

13
00:00:25,022 --> 00:00:28,105
every day for two weeks without recognizing

14
00:00:28,105 --> 00:00:28,392
her.

15
00:00:28,741 --> 00:00:30,966
Tonight I'm going to tell you what

16
00:00:30,966 --> 00:00:32,536
Helen saw in that house,

17
00:00:32,536 --> 00:00:35,219
how her daughter-in-law treated the help,

18
00:00:35,219 --> 00:00:37,378
and the one person in that family

19
00:00:37,378 --> 00:00:39,799
who saw through the disguise in about

20
00:00:39,799 --> 00:00:40,584
ten seconds.

21
00:00:40,764 --> 00:00:41,707
Stay with me,

22
00:00:41,707 --> 00:00:45,188
honey, because Christmas morning in that mansion

23
00:00:45,188 --> 00:00:48,090
was something nobody in that family will

24
00:00:48,090 --> 00:00:48,960
ever forget.

25
00:00:49,140 --> 00:00:51,010
Least of all her son.

26
00:00:51,359 --> 00:00:54,362
Helen Crawford and I went to Mansfield

27
00:00:54,362 --> 00:00:56,021
Senior High together,

28
00:00:56,021 --> 00:00:58,233
class of nineteen sixty-two.

29
00:00:58,413 --> 00:01:01,024
She married a man named Bill Crawford,

30
00:01:01,024 --> 00:01:03,772
who invented a little valve for furnaces

31
00:01:03,772 --> 00:01:06,383
and became a millionaire before he was

32
00:01:06,383 --> 00:01:06,795
forty.

33
00:01:06,975 --> 00:01:09,097
But Helen never got fancy,

34
00:01:09,097 --> 00:01:09,587
honey.

35
00:01:09,767 --> 00:01:12,089
She still shopped at the Kroger.

36
00:01:12,269 --> 00:01:14,312
She still came to my house for

37
00:01:14,312 --> 00:01:15,810
coffee every Thursday.

38
00:01:15,990 --> 00:01:17,768
And she had one son,

39
00:01:17,768 --> 00:01:20,879
Brian, whom she loved more than her

40
00:01:20,879 --> 00:01:21,679
own life.

41
00:01:22,030 --> 00:01:23,708
Brian went to Harvard,

42
00:01:23,708 --> 00:01:26,988
married a woman named Courtney from Boston,

43
00:01:26,988 --> 00:01:30,039
and moved into an enormous house outside

44
00:01:30,039 --> 00:01:30,725
Columbus.

45
00:01:30,905 --> 00:01:32,756
And little by little,

46
00:01:32,756 --> 00:01:35,840
honey, Helen stopped being invited.

47
00:01:36,020 --> 00:01:37,993
Courtney always had a reason.

48
00:01:38,173 --> 00:01:40,368
The house was being renovated.

49
00:01:40,548 --> 00:01:43,415
The children had too many activities.

50
00:01:43,595 --> 00:01:46,967
Christmas would be at Courtney's parents' this

51
00:01:46,967 --> 00:01:47,334
year.

52
00:01:47,514 --> 00:01:49,580
Four Christmases in a row.

53
00:01:49,760 --> 00:01:51,820
In a minute I'll tell you what

54
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:54,636
Helen found out Courtney had been telling

55
00:01:54,636 --> 00:01:56,215
the children about her.

56
00:01:56,566 --> 00:01:59,561
Helen had two grandchildren she barely knew.

57
00:01:59,741 --> 00:02:02,319
Emma, twelve, and Jack,

58
00:02:02,319 --> 00:02:02,992
seven.

59
00:02:03,172 --> 00:02:06,276
She sent presents every birthday and every

60
00:02:06,276 --> 00:02:07,015
Christmas.

61
00:02:07,195 --> 00:02:08,983
She never got a thank-you note.

62
00:02:09,163 --> 00:02:10,242
Not one.

63
00:02:10,422 --> 00:02:12,813
She'd sit at my kitchen table on

64
00:02:12,813 --> 00:02:14,158
Thursdays and say,

65
00:02:14,158 --> 00:02:16,249
Ruth, I just want to know if

66
00:02:16,249 --> 00:02:17,295
they're happy.

67
00:02:17,475 --> 00:02:19,786
I just want to see their faces.

68
00:02:19,966 --> 00:02:21,576
And I'd hold her hand,

69
00:02:21,576 --> 00:02:24,285
because there was nothing else to do.

70
00:02:24,635 --> 00:02:27,454
That November, Helen found out from a

71
00:02:27,454 --> 00:02:30,806
neighbor that Courtney was hiring extra help

72
00:02:30,806 --> 00:02:32,101
for the holidays,

73
00:02:32,101 --> 00:02:34,387
through an agency in Columbus.

74
00:02:34,567 --> 00:02:36,464
A maid to live in for two

75
00:02:36,464 --> 00:02:38,209
weeks before Christmas,

76
00:02:38,209 --> 00:02:39,954
while they had parties.

77
00:02:40,134 --> 00:02:42,279
And Helen came to my house with

78
00:02:42,279 --> 00:02:44,078
a look in her eye I hadn't

79
00:02:44,078 --> 00:02:46,569
seen since we were seventeen and she

80
00:02:46,569 --> 00:02:49,129
talked me into skipping school to see

81
00:02:49,129 --> 00:02:50,444
Elvis in Cleveland.

82
00:02:50,794 --> 00:02:52,289
She said, Ruth,

83
00:02:52,289 --> 00:02:54,184
I'm going to apply.

84
00:02:54,364 --> 00:02:55,228
And I said,

85
00:02:55,228 --> 00:02:58,767
Helen, you're seventy-two years old and worth

86
00:02:58,767 --> 00:03:00,575
thirty million dollars.

87
00:03:00,755 --> 00:03:02,022
And she said,

88
00:03:02,022 --> 00:03:05,724
I'm also the best housekeeper in Ohio.

89
00:03:05,904 --> 00:03:08,121
I cleaned my own house for forty

90
00:03:08,121 --> 00:03:10,615
years because Bill was too cheap for

91
00:03:10,615 --> 00:03:12,347
a maid until he was rich,

92
00:03:12,347 --> 00:03:14,286
and then I was too stubborn.

93
00:03:14,466 --> 00:03:17,578
Brian hasn't seen me in four years.

94
00:03:17,758 --> 00:03:18,989
I've gotten old.

95
00:03:19,169 --> 00:03:20,376
He won't know me.

96
00:03:20,726 --> 00:03:23,431
If you're enjoying Grandma's story,

97
00:03:23,431 --> 00:03:25,518
tap the like button for me,

98
00:03:25,518 --> 00:03:26,368
sweetheart.

99
00:03:26,548 --> 00:03:29,764
It helps more people find these stories.

100
00:03:29,944 --> 00:03:31,733
We bought a gray wig at the

101
00:03:31,733 --> 00:03:32,661
beauty supply.

102
00:03:32,841 --> 00:03:35,702
Helen's hair had been dyed auburn for

103
00:03:35,702 --> 00:03:36,707
thirty years.

104
00:03:36,887 --> 00:03:38,675
She took off her rings.

105
00:03:38,855 --> 00:03:41,165
She put on drugstore glasses.

106
00:03:41,345 --> 00:03:43,586
She called herself Mrs.

107
00:03:43,766 --> 00:03:45,194
Nell Davis.

108
00:03:45,374 --> 00:03:47,739
And I drove her to the agency

109
00:03:47,739 --> 00:03:50,839
myself, and I've never been so nervous

110
00:03:50,839 --> 00:03:51,736
in my life.

111
00:03:52,087 --> 00:03:53,805
She was hired on the spot.

112
00:03:53,985 --> 00:03:56,288
And on the first of December,

113
00:03:56,288 --> 00:03:59,307
Helen walked into her own son's house,

114
00:03:59,307 --> 00:04:01,054
through the back door,

115
00:04:01,054 --> 00:04:02,007
in an apron.

116
00:04:02,187 --> 00:04:04,554
Courtney met her in the kitchen and

117
00:04:04,554 --> 00:04:06,785
handed her a typed list of rules.

118
00:04:06,965 --> 00:04:08,915
Use the back stairs.

119
00:04:09,095 --> 00:04:12,288
Don't speak to guests unless spoken to.

120
00:04:12,468 --> 00:04:14,128
Don't touch the children.

121
00:04:14,308 --> 00:04:16,410
Eat in the laundry room.

122
00:04:16,590 --> 00:04:17,576
And she said,

123
00:04:17,576 --> 00:04:20,155
my mother-in-law used to be a maid

124
00:04:20,155 --> 00:04:21,141
too, I think.

125
00:04:21,321 --> 00:04:23,027
She'd fit right in.

126
00:04:23,378 --> 00:04:26,114
Brian came home at eight that night,

127
00:04:26,114 --> 00:04:26,570
honey.

128
00:04:26,750 --> 00:04:29,702
Helen was polishing silver in the dining

129
00:04:29,702 --> 00:04:30,071
room.

130
00:04:30,251 --> 00:04:32,245
He walked right past her,

131
00:04:32,245 --> 00:04:33,920
talking on his phone,

132
00:04:33,920 --> 00:04:35,835
and didn't even look up.

133
00:04:36,015 --> 00:04:37,397
His own mother.

134
00:04:37,577 --> 00:04:39,509
She told me later that her hands

135
00:04:39,509 --> 00:04:41,501
shook so hard she dropped a fork.

136
00:04:41,681 --> 00:04:43,210
She said, Ruth,

137
00:04:43,210 --> 00:04:44,943
he's gotten gray.

138
00:04:45,123 --> 00:04:46,853
He looks so tired.

139
00:04:47,033 --> 00:04:49,291
And he walked right past me like

140
00:04:49,291 --> 00:04:50,702
I was the furniture.

141
00:04:51,052 --> 00:04:52,282
For two weeks,

142
00:04:52,282 --> 00:04:54,128
Helen saw everything.

143
00:04:54,308 --> 00:04:56,270
Courtney screaming at the cook.

144
00:04:56,450 --> 00:04:59,833
Brian working until midnight and drinking alone

145
00:04:59,833 --> 00:05:00,769
in his study.

146
00:05:00,949 --> 00:05:03,753
The children eating dinner by themselves in

147
00:05:03,753 --> 00:05:05,187
front of a television.

148
00:05:05,367 --> 00:05:07,834
And she heard Courtney tell a friend

149
00:05:07,834 --> 00:05:09,273
at a Christmas party,

150
00:05:09,273 --> 00:05:10,301
Brian's mother?

151
00:05:10,481 --> 00:05:12,096
Oh, she's a dreadful,

152
00:05:12,096 --> 00:05:13,326
pushy old thing.

153
00:05:13,506 --> 00:05:14,806
She's not well.

154
00:05:14,986 --> 00:05:17,202
We keep the children away for their

155
00:05:17,202 --> 00:05:17,772
own good.

156
00:05:17,952 --> 00:05:20,112
That's what she'd been telling them.

157
00:05:20,462 --> 00:05:22,880
And every night at eleven o'clock,

158
00:05:22,880 --> 00:05:25,726
sweetheart, Helen would call me from the

159
00:05:25,726 --> 00:05:28,073
little phone in the laundry room,

160
00:05:28,073 --> 00:05:28,856
whispering.

161
00:05:29,036 --> 00:05:30,494
She'd say, Ruth,

162
00:05:30,494 --> 00:05:33,958
I scrubbed my own son's bathtub today.

163
00:05:34,138 --> 00:05:36,055
And he left a dollar on the

164
00:05:36,055 --> 00:05:37,119
counter for me,

165
00:05:37,119 --> 00:05:37,900
like a tip.

166
00:05:38,080 --> 00:05:38,893
A dollar.

167
00:05:39,073 --> 00:05:40,234
I kept it.

168
00:05:40,414 --> 00:05:42,004
I'm going to frame it.

169
00:05:42,184 --> 00:05:43,846
And then she'd laugh,

170
00:05:43,846 --> 00:05:45,349
and then she'd cry,

171
00:05:45,349 --> 00:05:47,722
and I'd tell her to come home.

172
00:05:47,902 --> 00:05:49,344
And every night,

173
00:05:49,344 --> 00:05:50,967
she said, not yet.

174
00:05:51,317 --> 00:05:52,737
So far, the wig,

175
00:05:52,737 --> 00:05:55,224
the rules, the laundry room,

176
00:05:55,224 --> 00:05:56,379
and Courtney.

177
00:05:56,559 --> 00:05:58,103
And then there was Jack.

178
00:05:58,283 --> 00:05:59,484
Jack was seven,

179
00:05:59,484 --> 00:06:01,725
with a cowlick and big ears,

180
00:06:01,725 --> 00:06:03,166
and he was lonely,

181
00:06:03,166 --> 00:06:03,646
honey.

182
00:06:03,826 --> 00:06:06,362
He'd wander into the kitchen at night

183
00:06:06,362 --> 00:06:08,213
when Helen was cleaning up,

184
00:06:08,213 --> 00:06:09,789
and sit on the counter,

185
00:06:09,789 --> 00:06:10,885
and talk to her.

186
00:06:11,065 --> 00:06:13,543
About dinosaurs, and his teacher,

187
00:06:13,543 --> 00:06:15,945
and how his daddy never had time

188
00:06:15,945 --> 00:06:16,545
to play.

189
00:06:16,725 --> 00:06:18,920
And Helen would give him a cookie

190
00:06:18,920 --> 00:06:20,649
she'd hidden in her apron.

191
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:22,483
On the fourth night,

192
00:06:22,483 --> 00:06:24,635
Jack looked at her for a long

193
00:06:24,635 --> 00:06:27,083
time while she washed the dishes.

194
00:06:27,263 --> 00:06:28,620
And then he said,

195
00:06:28,620 --> 00:06:31,095
Nell, why do you hum like that?

196
00:06:31,275 --> 00:06:34,408
Helen always hummed Silent Night when she

197
00:06:34,408 --> 00:06:35,477
washed dishes.

198
00:06:35,657 --> 00:06:37,503
She'd done it her whole life.

199
00:06:37,683 --> 00:06:38,894
And Jack said,

200
00:06:38,894 --> 00:06:41,143
my grandma hums like that.

201
00:06:41,323 --> 00:06:42,547
On the phone,

202
00:06:42,547 --> 00:06:43,959
at my birthday.

203
00:06:44,139 --> 00:06:47,022
Daddy lets me call her sometimes when

204
00:06:47,022 --> 00:06:48,190
Mom's not home.

205
00:06:48,370 --> 00:06:50,007
You sound just like her.

206
00:06:50,358 --> 00:06:52,855
And then that little boy looked at

207
00:06:52,855 --> 00:06:54,839
her hands in the dishwater,

208
00:06:54,839 --> 00:06:55,721
and he said,

209
00:06:55,721 --> 00:06:58,219
very quietly, you have a ring mark

210
00:06:58,219 --> 00:06:59,320
on your finger.

211
00:06:59,500 --> 00:07:01,893
Grandma has a ring with a green

212
00:07:01,893 --> 00:07:02,357
stone.

213
00:07:02,537 --> 00:07:04,185
Are you my grandma?

214
00:07:04,365 --> 00:07:06,629
Helen said she couldn't breathe.

215
00:07:06,809 --> 00:07:09,665
She took off her drugstore glasses.

216
00:07:09,845 --> 00:07:12,520
And Jack threw his arms around her

217
00:07:12,520 --> 00:07:13,621
neck and said,

218
00:07:13,621 --> 00:07:14,408
I knew it.

219
00:07:14,588 --> 00:07:15,679
I knew it.

220
00:07:15,859 --> 00:07:17,252
I won't tell.

221
00:07:17,432 --> 00:07:19,476
I promise I won't tell.

222
00:07:19,826 --> 00:07:21,251
For the next week,

223
00:07:21,251 --> 00:07:24,261
Jack and his grandmother had a secret,

224
00:07:24,261 --> 00:07:24,737
honey.

225
00:07:24,917 --> 00:07:27,418
Every night, he'd sneak down to the

226
00:07:27,418 --> 00:07:30,349
kitchen, and she'd tell him stories about

227
00:07:30,349 --> 00:07:32,208
when his daddy was little.

228
00:07:32,388 --> 00:07:34,806
How Brian was afraid of the dark

229
00:07:34,806 --> 00:07:36,091
until he was ten.

230
00:07:36,271 --> 00:07:38,301
How he cried at the end of

231
00:07:38,301 --> 00:07:39,940
every Lassie episode.

232
00:07:40,120 --> 00:07:42,518
How he'd saved up his allowance for

233
00:07:42,518 --> 00:07:44,573
a whole year to buy his mother

234
00:07:44,573 --> 00:07:46,355
a music box for Christmas.

235
00:07:46,535 --> 00:07:49,658
A music box that played Silent Night.

236
00:07:50,008 --> 00:07:52,364
And Jack told her things too.

237
00:07:52,544 --> 00:07:55,346
That his mother said Grandma was mean,

238
00:07:55,346 --> 00:07:58,000
and sick, and wanted to take Daddy's

239
00:07:58,000 --> 00:07:58,442
money.

240
00:07:58,622 --> 00:08:00,545
That Daddy cried once,

241
00:08:00,545 --> 00:08:01,682
in his study,

242
00:08:01,682 --> 00:08:03,867
holding an old music box,

243
00:08:03,867 --> 00:08:06,053
and Jack didn't know why.

244
00:08:06,233 --> 00:08:08,969
And Helen sat at that kitchen island

245
00:08:08,969 --> 00:08:10,869
in her apron at midnight,

246
00:08:10,869 --> 00:08:12,997
holding her grandson's hand,

247
00:08:12,997 --> 00:08:16,113
and realized her son wasn't happy either.

248
00:08:16,293 --> 00:08:19,017
Not one person in that big house

249
00:08:19,017 --> 00:08:19,869
was happy.

250
00:08:20,219 --> 00:08:22,675
Two nights before Christmas,

251
00:08:22,675 --> 00:08:24,255
Helen almost quit,

252
00:08:24,255 --> 00:08:24,781
honey.

253
00:08:24,961 --> 00:08:27,492
Courtney found a cookie crumb on Jack's

254
00:08:27,492 --> 00:08:29,894
pajamas and screamed at Nell in front

255
00:08:29,894 --> 00:08:32,749
of everybody, for feeding the children sugar

256
00:08:32,749 --> 00:08:33,982
without permission.

257
00:08:34,162 --> 00:08:36,624
She called her a useless old woman

258
00:08:36,624 --> 00:08:39,085
and threatened to call the agency.

259
00:08:39,265 --> 00:08:41,502
Helen stood there in her apron and

260
00:08:41,502 --> 00:08:42,028
took it.

261
00:08:42,208 --> 00:08:44,803
And when Courtney left the room,

262
00:08:44,803 --> 00:08:46,911
Emma, the twelve-year-old,

263
00:08:46,911 --> 00:08:49,830
quietly brought her a glass of water

264
00:08:49,830 --> 00:08:52,587
and said, I'm sorry my mom yelled.

265
00:08:52,938 --> 00:08:56,572
That night, Helen called me and said,

266
00:08:56,572 --> 00:08:58,046
Ruth, I'm done.

267
00:08:58,226 --> 00:08:59,410
I've seen enough.

268
00:08:59,590 --> 00:09:01,396
I'll leave in the morning,

269
00:09:01,396 --> 00:09:03,201
and I'll never tell Brian.

270
00:09:03,381 --> 00:09:04,971
It would only hurt him.

271
00:09:05,151 --> 00:09:06,173
And I said,

272
00:09:06,173 --> 00:09:08,309
Helen, what about Jack?

273
00:09:08,489 --> 00:09:10,335
What about Christmas morning?

274
00:09:10,515 --> 00:09:12,763
And there was a long silence on

275
00:09:12,763 --> 00:09:13,488
the phone.

276
00:09:13,668 --> 00:09:15,016
And then she said,

277
00:09:15,016 --> 00:09:15,990
you're right.

278
00:09:16,170 --> 00:09:18,588
That little boy is keeping a secret

279
00:09:18,588 --> 00:09:19,072
for me.

280
00:09:19,252 --> 00:09:21,748
I can't leave him holding it alone.

281
00:09:22,098 --> 00:09:23,433
On Christmas morning,

282
00:09:23,433 --> 00:09:26,676
Courtney had arranged everything for a photographer

283
00:09:26,676 --> 00:09:27,694
from a magazine.

284
00:09:27,874 --> 00:09:29,929
The tree was twenty feet tall.

285
00:09:30,109 --> 00:09:33,150
Everybody was in matching red sweaters.

286
00:09:33,330 --> 00:09:35,478
And Helen was in the corner in

287
00:09:35,478 --> 00:09:38,056
her apron, holding a tray of coffee.

288
00:09:38,236 --> 00:09:40,178
And then Jack stood up in the

289
00:09:40,178 --> 00:09:42,724
middle of all those presents and said,

290
00:09:42,724 --> 00:09:44,935
wait, I have a present for Daddy.

291
00:09:45,115 --> 00:09:47,098
And he ran to the kitchen and

292
00:09:47,098 --> 00:09:49,561
came back pulling Helen by the hand.

293
00:09:49,912 --> 00:09:52,043
He stood her in front of his

294
00:09:52,043 --> 00:09:54,630
father, and he pulled off her gray

295
00:09:54,630 --> 00:09:55,924
wig, and he said,

296
00:09:55,924 --> 00:09:57,446
Daddy, it's Grandma.

297
00:09:57,626 --> 00:09:59,716
She's been here the whole time.

298
00:09:59,896 --> 00:10:02,207
You walked past her every day.

299
00:10:02,387 --> 00:10:04,813
The photographer stopped clicking.

300
00:10:04,993 --> 00:10:07,025
Courtney dropped her coffee cup.

301
00:10:07,205 --> 00:10:09,190
And Brian stood up slowly,

302
00:10:09,190 --> 00:10:12,167
honey, and stared at his mother's face,

303
00:10:12,167 --> 00:10:14,839
with her auburn hair falling out of

304
00:10:14,839 --> 00:10:15,984
the bobby pins,

305
00:10:15,984 --> 00:10:17,816
and his face fell apart.

306
00:10:18,167 --> 00:10:19,653
He said, Mom?

307
00:10:19,833 --> 00:10:21,690
Mom, you were the maid?

308
00:10:21,870 --> 00:10:23,230
And Helen said,

309
00:10:23,230 --> 00:10:25,042
I wanted to see you,

310
00:10:25,042 --> 00:10:25,585
Brian.

311
00:10:25,765 --> 00:10:27,688
And I wanted to know if you

312
00:10:27,688 --> 00:10:28,471
were happy.

313
00:10:28,651 --> 00:10:29,568
You're not.

314
00:10:29,748 --> 00:10:30,714
And her son,

315
00:10:30,714 --> 00:10:32,485
forty-eight years old,

316
00:10:32,485 --> 00:10:34,980
a Harvard man in a matching red

317
00:10:34,980 --> 00:10:37,959
sweater, put his face in his mother's

318
00:10:37,959 --> 00:10:40,696
apron and cried like a little boy.

319
00:10:40,876 --> 00:10:43,044
In front of the photographer,

320
00:10:43,044 --> 00:10:44,764
and Courtney's parents,

321
00:10:44,764 --> 00:10:45,810
and everybody.

322
00:10:46,161 --> 00:10:48,774
Courtney screamed that it was a trick,

323
00:10:48,774 --> 00:10:50,493
and that Helen was crazy,

324
00:10:50,493 --> 00:10:52,418
just like she'd always said.

325
00:10:52,598 --> 00:10:53,736
But Jack said,

326
00:10:53,736 --> 00:10:57,231
Mommy, you told everybody Grandma was mean.

327
00:10:57,411 --> 00:10:58,305
She's not.

328
00:10:58,485 --> 00:11:01,402
She's the nicest person in this whole

329
00:11:01,402 --> 00:11:01,875
house.

330
00:11:02,055 --> 00:11:04,249
And twelve-year-old Emma,

331
00:11:04,249 --> 00:11:07,057
who'd been quiet the whole time,

332
00:11:07,057 --> 00:11:08,461
said, it's true,

333
00:11:08,461 --> 00:11:08,812
Mom.

334
00:11:08,992 --> 00:11:10,617
You told us she was sick.

335
00:11:10,797 --> 00:11:11,912
You lied.

336
00:11:12,092 --> 00:11:14,932
Nobody in that room could argue with

337
00:11:14,932 --> 00:11:15,958
two children.

338
00:11:16,308 --> 00:11:19,319
Brian and Courtney separated that spring,

339
00:11:19,319 --> 00:11:20,127
sweetheart.

340
00:11:20,307 --> 00:11:22,380
I won't say more about that,

341
00:11:22,380 --> 00:11:24,452
because it's their business.

342
00:11:24,632 --> 00:11:27,357
But Brian moved back to Mansfield with

343
00:11:27,357 --> 00:11:28,289
the children.

344
00:11:28,469 --> 00:11:31,012
He took a job running his father's

345
00:11:31,012 --> 00:11:32,359
old valve company.

346
00:11:32,539 --> 00:11:34,288
And every Thursday now,

347
00:11:34,288 --> 00:11:36,798
he brings Helen to my kitchen for

348
00:11:36,798 --> 00:11:39,612
coffee, and Jack eats all my cookies,

349
00:11:39,612 --> 00:11:41,513
and I let him have three.

350
00:11:41,863 --> 00:11:43,939
Helen is eighty-two now,

351
00:11:43,939 --> 00:11:45,497
honey, same as me.

352
00:11:45,677 --> 00:11:47,174
Jack is seventeen.

353
00:11:47,354 --> 00:11:49,120
And every Christmas Eve,

354
00:11:49,120 --> 00:11:51,179
he puts on that gray wig and

355
00:11:51,179 --> 00:11:53,901
an apron and serves everybody coffee,

356
00:11:53,901 --> 00:11:56,696
and the whole family laughs until they

357
00:11:56,696 --> 00:11:56,991
cry.

358
00:11:57,171 --> 00:12:00,011
And on Helen's nightstand is a music

359
00:12:00,011 --> 00:12:02,221
box that plays Silent Night.

360
00:12:02,401 --> 00:12:05,257
Brian kept it for forty years.

361
00:12:05,437 --> 00:12:07,546
He gave it back to her that

362
00:12:07,546 --> 00:12:08,328
Christmas.

363
00:12:08,678 --> 00:12:11,425
Here's what I want you to remember,

364
00:12:11,425 --> 00:12:12,288
sweetheart.

365
00:12:12,468 --> 00:12:14,872
Children always know the truth.

366
00:12:15,052 --> 00:12:17,188
Listen to the quiet ones.

367
00:12:17,368 --> 00:12:19,301
Tell me in the comments,

368
00:12:19,301 --> 00:12:22,282
honey, would you have done what Helen

369
00:12:22,282 --> 00:12:22,604
did?

370
00:12:22,784 --> 00:12:25,223
There are more of my family's stories

371
00:12:25,223 --> 00:12:27,531
in the playlist right there on your

372
00:12:27,531 --> 00:12:29,575
screen, and I'll see you in the

373
00:12:29,575 --> 00:12:30,168
next one.

374
00:12:30,348 --> 00:12:31,184
Good night.

375
00:12:31,364 --> 00:12:32,757
Merry Christmas.
